====================================================================================================
SECTION 7 -- THE INDEPENDENT 2 s BY-NAME SAMPLER BESIDE RUN 6
====================================================================================================

379 lines of `S <uptime> z18=<raw> z12=<raw> z9=<raw> g0=<governor> f0=<cur_freq>`, every value
read BY NAME from /sys/class/thermal/thermal_zone{18,12,9}/temp -- no column arithmetic, nothing
derived from the instrument. It ran in a second ssh session beside the run.

(a) THE WRITE HELD, AND IT WAS THE WRITE THAT CHANGED THE CLOCK. Splitting the sampler at its own
    governor column into state periods:

    state        span (uptime s)      s   n   z18 mean  z12 mean  z9 mean
    interactive [29744,29791]    47  24     43.69     43.71    43.30
    performance [29793,29878]    84  43     44.71     46.67    45.49
    interactive [29880,29977]    96  48     44.25     44.62    44.19
    performance [29979,30020]    40  21     44.48     46.40    45.67

    Two states only, and in 84 s of `performance` the little cores' cur_freq never moved off
    1440000 -- the cap -- while in `interactive` it moved between 307200 and 1363200. So the two
    halves differ in the clock, not just in the name of a file.

(b) THE WINDOW MEANS. Six window spans, each 48 s of state (the design's 30 s window plus its 5 s
    settle plus this instrument's walk), split at the midpoints of the two merged same-state runs:

    window   zone18  zone12   zone9
    A.1       43.69   43.71   43.30
    B.1       44.35   46.53   45.40
    B.2       45.09   46.82   45.59
    A.2       44.35   44.93   44.38
    A.3       44.15   44.32   43.99
    B.3       44.58   46.46   45.80

    The SAME pairing, on the same runs, with window MEANS instead of the instrument's snapshot:

      zone18   d_i = [0.66, 0.74, 0.43]   mean +0.61  se 0.09  interval [+0.42, +0.80]
      zone12   d_i = [2.82, 1.89, 2.15]   mean +2.29  se 0.28  interval [+1.73, +2.84]
      zone9    d_i = [2.1, 1.21, 1.81]   mean +1.71  se 0.26  interval [+1.19, +2.23]

    The instrument, on the same run, read [+2.30, +6.5-ish, +5-ish] on those three zones -- ONE SNAPSHOT of each window,
    taken at the END of the window (zl1-thermal.sh prints its header, sleeps the window, walks
    /proc, and only THEN reads the zones -- the header is the START and the zone table is the END).

    Every value the instrument printed is IN the sampler's series; the two disagree because a
    window whose zone moves 42.4 -> 45.6 -> 43.7 inside 48 s (zone18, run 6) has no single value.
    On zone18 the two legitimate readings of the SAME run differ by 1.7 C -- three quarters of the
    effect being priced.

(c) THE CAP IS A CONSTANT OF THE IMAGE, NOT OF THE TEMPERATURE. `scaling_max_freq` read every 4 s
    for 4 minutes while the phone cooled from 48 to 41 C on zone12:

    max=[1440000 1440000 1900800 1900800] -- one distinct value in 60 samples; cpuinfo_max_freq
    is 2188800 / 2342400. There are NO cooling devices under /sys/class/thermal/. So `performance`
    means 1440000/1900800 on this image, and it is what both halves were held at.

(d) ONE TSENS ZONE MOVES ~5 C IN SECONDS AT IDLE. The same by-name reads, 4 s apart, on an idle
    phone: z12 = 412, 480, 406, 429, 467, 429 ... while z18 stayed inside 420..437. That is the
    zone the instrument called the primary on runs 2 and 3.


====================================================================================================
SECTION 8 -- THE INSTRUMENT IS NOT MISREADING THE ZONES (checked twice, by hand, by name)
====================================================================================================
Two spot checks, each a `zl1-thermal.sh` window running beside by-name reads of the same file, and
in the second one the column sampler was ALSO compared by name (`${11}` this time, not `$11`, which
dash parses as `${1}1` -- that is how one of my own probes printed 350001 for a temperature):

  zl1-thermal.sh --seconds 30, header uptime 29644, printed thermal_zone18 = 42.7 C
     by-name at 29644.00, 1 s apart:  42.0  43.0  44.9  ...   43.0  (the header is the START of
     the window, so the instrument's zone read is ~40 s later, inside the same band)
  zl1-thermal.sh --seconds 20, header uptime 29696, printed thermal_zone18 = 44.9 C
     by-name at 29697.06 / 29698.23 / 29699.35 / 29700.53:  43.7  44.3  43.3  44.6
     same instants, column 11 (`${11}`):                    43.3  44.0  43.3  44.6

So the zone read is right, the unit table is right, and the two readers agree to a sample. What they
do NOT agree on, during a run, is which INSTANT represents a window -- section 7(b).

====================================================================================================
WHAT WAS NOT DONE
====================================================================================================
Nothing was flashed. No partition was written. /dev/qbt1000 was never opened. cnss was never
unbound. No service was restarted and the container was never stopped. The only writes this stage
made to the phone were `interactive`/`performance` into the four cores' scaling_governor, which is
what the instrument does by design, each written back to `interactive` by an EXIT trap and re-read.
The four cores read `interactive` after every one of the six runs, and the boot_id is the one the
boot started with.
